---
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Author from '../../components/Author.astro';

---

<Layout title="What's new in Yap: movie clips, an iOS app, books, and Anki decks" description="Movie clips, a native iOS and iPadOS app, a books tab, Anki deck export, and a lot of new languages.">
  <article class="prose prose-invert [&_code]:before:content-none [&_code]:after:content-none">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-2">What's new in Yap</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-8">October 25, 2026</p>
    <p>
      Here's a roundup of the new features in Yap.Town. As always, Yap remains 100% free with no ads, and <a href="https://github.com/yaptown/yap">the source code is on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's been 7 months since the last update, so there are quite a few new features to go through 😁
    </p>

    <h2>Movie clips!</h2>
    <p>
      Yap helps you practice your language by showing you sentences from movies. Now, when possible, it will also show you the actual scene the sentence is from!
    </p>
    <img src="/blog/october-2026-update-clip.webp" alt="A Yap listening exercise showing a clip from The Umbrellas of Cherbourg" class="mx-auto max-w-md w-full rounded-lg" />
    <p>
      Not every sentence has a movie clip, but there are over 100k clips overall, so you still see them pretty regularly.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want to learn more about how to legally source 100k movie clips, <a href="/blog/french-professor-loophole/">read the blog post</a>!
    </p>

    <h2>iOS and iPadOS apps!</h2>
    <p>
      There is now a native iOS and iPadOS app! Nerd stuff below:
    </p>
    <p>
      Normally, maintaining a native iOS and web experience would be very difficult. For most apps, it would require writing the app twice. However, Yap is not most apps. Most of its logic is written in the programming language Rust, which is compiled to WASM to run in the browser. JavaScript is then only used for the UI. For the iOS version, that same Rust is compiled to native code, and Swift is used for the UI. This means that most of the codebase is shared between the two versions! Without this, it would be incredibly difficult to keep both versions in sync. (More on this in <a href="/blog/maintaining-two-apps/">How I maintain two apps</a>.)
    </p>

    <h2>Books</h2>
    <p>
      There's a new tab in Yap where you can read some books in your target language. You can tap on any word to see its definition.
    </p>
    <img src="/blog/october-2026-update-books.webp" alt="The Books tab for French: Arsène Lupin, Le Tour du monde en quatre-vingts jours, and Mistress of the Waves" class="mx-auto max-w-sm w-full rounded-lg" />
    <p>
      Every language has two public domain books, plus <em>Mistress of the Waves</em> by George Phillies. Who is George Phillies, you ask?
    </p>
    <p>
      He's an interesting guy. He studied at MIT and eventually became a Professor of Physics at WPI. He also ran for president. But after more than 170 technical papers and 51 years of academia, he's now retired to write fiction full-time. And he's generously agreed to let me use <em>Mistress of the Waves</em> in Yap.Town. It's a great book about a conscientious sailor in a strange world, and it's been translated into every language Yap offers.
    </p>

    <h2>Anki decks</h2>
    <p>
      You can now export an Anki deck with words and sentences from Yap! You can try it at <a href="/anki">yap.town/anki</a>. It's based on your current progress, so if you ever decide you're sick of Yap, you can get an Anki deck with the same cards Yap would have given you next. (The Anki deck even has movie clips!)
    </p>
    <img src="/blog/october-2026-update-anki.webp" alt="A Japanese card from an exported deck in AnkiMobile, with a clip from The Wind Rises" class="mx-auto max-w-xs w-full rounded-lg" />

    <h2>New languages</h2>
    <p>
      Yap now supports the following languages:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>French</li>
      <li>Latin American Spanish</li>
      <li>Peninsular Spanish</li>
      <li>German</li>
      <li>Italian</li>
      <li>Brazilian Portuguese</li>
      <li>European Portuguese</li>
      <li>Russian</li>
      <li>Korean</li>
      <li>Japanese</li>
      <li>Chinese (Simplified)</li>
      <li>Hindi</li>
      <li>Thai</li>
      <li>English (for French speakers)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Japanese was a bit tricky to add. It's tricky because Japanese characters don't have a consistent sound. Let me explain with an example:
    </p>
    <p>
      We kind of have three different words for water in English. You can say "water", "hydro", or "aqua". Like, if you say "hydroelectricity", it's clearly water-electricity, or if you say "aquatic", it's "water-living". Even though the meaning is the same, the spelling and pronunciation are different. In Japanese, words like this are still pronounced differently, just like in English, but they're written with the same character. So even if you know the character means "water", there's no way to tell the pronunciation just by looking at the word.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anyway, I eventually figured out a system that uses Japanese dictionaries and LLMs to figure out the pronunciation of each word in each sentence in Yap's database. This allows Yap to teach you one pronunciation at a time, rather than confusing you by showing sentences where the same character is pronounced differently.
    </p>

    <h2>A bit about me :P</h2>
    <p>
      Thanks for reading this far. If you ever want to chat, you're welcome to email me at <a href="mailto:andre@popovit.ch">andre@popovit.ch</a> or message me on <a href="https://x.com/ChadNauseam">Twitter/X</a>. I have a new day job now too. I'm doing software engineering for a small San Francisco robotics startup. It's pretty fun, I get to train models and write code that controls some really expensive robots.
    </p>
  </article>
  <Author />
</Layout>
